---
// src/components/EducationIslandSection.astro
//
// Same family as ArtIslandSection.astro / ComputerIslandSection.astro:
// two illustrations flanking centered-ish text, one JS-driven
// mouse-repel script, one CSS bob loop per island. This is a
// "background + foreground" pair rather than two symmetric corners,
// and both live on the RIGHT side, stacked on top of each other:
//
//   - `bigImage`   (education_island.svg) -> the large landmass,
//     sitting low-right, further back visually (lower z-index,
//     positioned first in the DOM). Sized/positioned so it just
//     slightly overlaps into the text column — `.content` stays
//     z-index 3 (above both islands) so the text is never actually
//     obscured, just visually close to the island's edge.
//   - `smallImage` (small_island.svg)     -> the smaller island
//     carrying the college-building silhouette, layered in front of
//     the big island (higher z-index), sitting a bit higher up and
//     inset from it so it reads as "on top of" the mountain rather
//     than off to another corner.
//
// Background: real image (`backgroundImage`), via `background-image`
// + `background-size: cover`, `min-height: 100vh` — same technique
// every section in this family uses. Every section keeps a CONSISTENT
// height across every screen size, with the background's left/right
// edges cropped as needed to make that happen.
//
// `background-position: bottom` protects the true bottom edge — the
// one that has to match AchievementIslandSection's top — from ever
// being cropped; `cover` is free to crop the top and the sides.
//
// Carries `seam-fade-top` (blending into var(--intro-seam)) against
// IntroIslandSection's bottom — updated after you moved this section
// to sit right after Intro instead of after Computer. This used to
// target var(--sky-2) (matching ComputerIslandSection's bottom, back
// when Computer was this section's neighbor); with the new order that
// pairing no longer exists, so the two sides disagreed on a color and
// the seam showed as a hard line instead of a blend. Retargeting to
// var(--intro-seam) matches what's now actually above this section
// (the same variable IntroIslandSection's own seam-fade-bottom uses).
//
// Also carries `seam-fade-bottom` (blending into var(--sky-3)) against
// ArtIslandSection's top — unchanged variable, but now blending
// against a DIFFERENT section than before (Art used to sit right after
// Intro; now it sits right after Education instead). Confirmed via
// sampling the actual rendered screenshot that var(--sky-3) is still a
// solid match for this new pairing — very close to both Education's
// real bottom-edge color and Art's real top-edge color — so no change
// was needed here; only the top side broke. ArtIslandSection's own
// `--seam-target-top` was updated to match (see the comment there).
//
// Positioning: `.education-island-stack`'s `top`/`right` use the
// clamp(mobileValue, calc(a + b*vw), desktopValue) fluid technique
// from ArtIslandSection.astro, since it bleeds off the right edge on
// wide screens (a fixed rem offset doesn't scale with viewport width
// the way a vw-based width does — see the longer explanation in
// ArtIslandSection.astro). On mobile the stack is pushed even further
// right so part of it is deliberately cropped off-screen — see the
// max-width: 640px query.
//
// `smallImage` is positioned in %, relative to the stack — NOT vh/vw
// relative to the section. That's a deliberate fix: `bigImage`'s
// rendered height depends on its own width (it's `width: <something
// in vw>; height: auto`), so its actual pixel height changes with
// viewport width in a way that has nothing to do with vh. Positioning
// `smallImage` with an independent vh offset meant the two only lined
// up correctly at whatever viewport width they happened to be tuned
// against — everywhere else, `smallImage` drifted up or down the
// mountain because the two offsets (vh vs. the big island's actual
// vw-driven height) scaled at different rates. Since `.education-
// island-stack` is sized to `bigImage`'s own rendered box (nothing
// else inside it takes up space), a percentage-based top/right on
// `smallImage` is always relative to that same box, so it scales in
// lockstep with `bigImage` at every viewport width instead of just a
// couple of tuned ones.
// These are starting values eyeballed off the screenshots — expect to
// nudge them once you see it live, same as the other two sections.
//
// Still carries `.sky-chain`, so the gap-collapsing on both sides
// keeps working regardless of what backs it.
//
// Now also carries `.halftone-hover`, joining the single, page-wide
// cursor-spotlight element in Layout.astro (`.halftone-spotlight`,
// see the comment there) purely by having this class — no markup or
// script needed here for it, since `body:has(.halftone-hover:hover)
// .halftone-spotlight` is what actually turns the spotlight on.
//
// NOTE: I still don't have the actual background image files — this
// is reasoned from first principles, not measured against the real
// files.
//
// Usage:
//   <EducationIslandSection
//     id="section-education"
//     eyebrow="Island 03"
//     title="Adipiscing Elit Sed"
//     text="Sunt in culpa qui officia deserunt mollit anim id est laborum..."
//     bigImage="/images/education_island.svg"
//     smallImage="/images/small_island.svg"
//     backgroundImage="/images/education_island_background.webp"
//   />

interface Props {
  id: string;
  eyebrow?: string;
  title: string;
  text: string;
  bigImage: string;
  smallImage: string;
  backgroundImage: string;
}

const { id, eyebrow, title, text, bigImage, smallImage, backgroundImage } = Astro.props;
---

<section
  class="education-section sky-chain seam-fade-top seam-fade-bottom halftone-hover"
  id={id}
  style={`background-image: url(${backgroundImage});`}
>
  <div class="education-island-stack" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="education-island-big">
      <div class="education-island-inner parallax-inner">
        <img src={bigImage} alt="" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="education-island-small">
      <div class="education-island-inner parallax-inner">
        <img src={smallImage} alt="" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content">
    {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
    <h2>{title}</h2>
    <p class="body-text">{text}</p>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  // Same proximity-repel drift as the rest of this family, driving
  // every `.parallax-inner` element on the page. If more than one of
  // these sections lands on the same page, each ships its own copy of
  // this script and each queries ALL `.parallax-inner` elements
  // globally (not just its own) — intentional/harmless (they all
  // compute the same easing toward the same targets), just a little
  // redundant. Worth hoisting into one shared script if this family
  // keeps growing.
  const islands = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".parallax-inner");
  const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia(
    "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
  ).matches;

  if (islands.length && !prefersReducedMotion) {
    const maxOffset = 36; // px, strongest push when cursor is right on top of the island
    const influenceRadius = 500; // px, push fades to 0 beyond this distance
    const ease = 0.08;

    let mouseX = 0;
    let mouseY = 0;
    let hasMouse = false;

    const state = Array.from(islands).map((el) => ({
      el,
      currentX: 0,
      currentY: 0,
    }));

    window.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
      mouseX = e.clientX;
      mouseY = e.clientY;
      hasMouse = true;
    });

    function tick() {
      for (const s of state) {
        let targetX = 0;
        let targetY = 0;

        if (hasMouse) {
          const rect = s.el.getBoundingClientRect();
          const restX = rect.left + rect.width / 2 - s.currentX;
          const restY = rect.top + rect.height / 2 - s.currentY;

          const dx = restX - mouseX;
          const dy = restY - mouseY;
          const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1;

          if (dist < influenceRadius) {
            const falloff = 1 - dist / influenceRadius;
            const nx = dx / dist;
            const ny = dy / dist;
            targetX = nx * maxOffset * falloff;
            targetY = ny * maxOffset * falloff;
          }
        }

        s.currentX += (targetX - s.currentX) * ease;
        s.currentY += (targetY - s.currentY) * ease;
        s.el.style.transform = `translate(${s.currentX.toFixed(2)}px, ${s.currentY.toFixed(2)}px)`;
      }
      requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    }

    requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  }
</script>

<style>
  .education-section {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100vh;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 7rem) clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    /* No `overflow: hidden` here — see the comment on `overflow-x:
       hidden` on `html` in Layout.astro. That handles horizontal
       clipping page-wide (including the island stack's intentional
       bleed off the right edge), so the stack isn't hard-clipped
       vertically when it needs more room than this section provides. */
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: bottom;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    /* Seam-blend targets (see the shared comment in Layout.astro) —
       one variable per edge since this section needs two different
       colors. Top softens into var(--intro-seam) (IntroIslandSection's
       bottom, now that this section sits right after Intro); bottom
       softens into var(--sky-3) (ArtIslandSection's top, now that Art
       sits right after this section instead of Achievement). */
    --seam-target-top: var(--intro-seam);
    --seam-target-bottom: var(--sky-3);
  }

  .content {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 3;

    width: min(32ch, 100%);
    margin: 0 auto;

    text-align: left;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #555;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: #1a1a1a;
  }

  .body-text {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: #444;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* Positioned against the section: bleeds off the right edge on
     wide screens, so `right` keeps the fluid
     clamp(mobileValue, calc(a + b*vw), desktopValue) treatment.
     Anchors: -2rem at 640px viewport width, 3rem at 1280px. Sized to
     `bigImage`'s own width — nothing else inside it affects its size
     — which is exactly what makes it a stable coordinate system for
     `.education-island-small`'s percentage-based position below.
     `pointer-events: none` lives here now instead of on `.education-
     island`, since that covers both children at once. */
  .education-island-stack {
    position: absolute;
    top: 16vh;
    right: clamp(-2rem, calc(-112px + 12.5vw), 3rem);
    width: clamp(390px, 46vw, 700px);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .education-island-inner img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* Big background landmass: fills the stack (100% of its width — the
     stack was sized to this image in the first place), normal
     document flow inside it (not absolutely positioned — it's the
     thing the stack's own size comes from). `.content` is z-index 3,
     above both islands, so the text itself never gets covered even
     where the stack's edge slightly overlaps the text column — it
     just sits visually close to it. z-index 1 = the back layer. */
  .education-island-big {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    width: 100%;
    animation: bob-big 7.5s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  /* Small foreground island with the college silhouette: positioned in
     % relative to the stack (i.e. relative to the big island's own
     rendered box), NOT vh/vw relative to the section — see the
     component-level comment above for why that's the fix for it
     drifting out of place across viewport widths. `top`/`right`/
     `width` are all % of the stack, so they scale in lockstep with
     the big island automatically. These are eyeballed starting values
     for "sits on the lower-middle of the mountain" — nudge directly if
     it needs to move. z-index 2 = in front of the big island. */
  .education-island-small {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 2;
    top: 86%;
    right: 18%;
    width: 30%;
    animation: bob-small 6s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  @keyframes bob-big {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    50% { transform: translateY(-8px); }
  }

  @keyframes bob-small {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    50% { transform: translateY(-10px); }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .education-island-big,
    .education-island-small {
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .content {
      width: min(32ch, 100%);
      padding-inline: 1.5rem;
    }

    /* Deliberately push the whole stack further right than its
       desktop position so part of it crops off-screen — same
       overflow: hidden that protects against horizontal scrolling
       elsewhere in this family, just leaned into on purpose here. */
    .education-island-stack {
      right: -8vw;
    }
  }
</style>